[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Type Soundness Pitfalls and Structural Typing (Duck Typing) in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript utilizes a structural type system, often referred to as duck typing. Unlike nominal type systems found in languages like Java or C# where compatibility is based on explicit declarations, TypeScript evaluates type compatibility based solely on the presence and structure of properties. While this provides great flexibility, it introduces specific type soundness pitfalls that developers must navigate."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Structural Typing"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a structurally typed system, if two objects share the same shape, they are considered to be of the same type. Let us examine a basic example to illustrate how TypeScript evaluates compatibility."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"interface Point2D {
  x: number;
  y: number;
}

let point: Point2D = { x: 10, y: 20 };

let point3D = { x: 10, y: 20, z: 30 };

// Valid because point3D has at least x and y
point = point3D;"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Notice that assigning point3D to point is entirely valid. The target type (Point2D) only requires x and y. Because point3D contains those properties plus an extra one (z), it is structurally compatible."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"The Pitfall of Excess Property Checks"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript catches unexpected properties when you pass object literals directly into functions or variable declarations. However, it bypasses this check if you pass an existing object reference. This behavior is a common source of confusion."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"interface Options {
  darkMode: boolean;
}

function configure(opts: Options) {
  console.log(opts);
}

// Error: Object literal may only specify known properties
// configure({ darkMode: true, extraProperty: 42 });

// This works without error because of structural typing
const userOptions = { darkMode: true, extraProperty: 42 };
configure(userOptions);"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Type Soundness Pitfall: Freshness and Function Parameters"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Fresh object literals undergo excess property checking to prevent typos and invalid configurations. Once an object ceases to be "fresh" (i.e., it is assigned to a variable), structural typing takes full control, allowing extra properties to slip through silently."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fresh object literals trigger strict checks for unrecognized properties."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Referenced objects only require a superset of the target type's properties."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Optional properties can sometimes mask missing runtime implementations if not strictly validated."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Mitigating Soundness Gaps with Brand Types"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"To enforce nominal typing behavior in TypeScript and avoid unintended structural substitutions, developers use a pattern called "branding" or "nominal tagging"."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"type USD = number & { readonly __brand: "USD" };
type EUR = number & { readonly __brand: "EUR" };

function createUSD(amount: number): USD {
  return amount as USD;
}

let walletUSD = createUSD(100);
let walletEUR = 100 as EUR;

// Error: Type "EUR" is not assignable to type "USD"
// walletUSD = walletEUR;"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Structural typing is a powerful feature that makes JavaScript integration seamless, but it sacrifices strict type soundness in favor of flexibility. Understanding object freshness, excess property checks, and utilizing techniques like branding helps maintain robust, bug-free TypeScript applications."}]
